    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The Government of India’s Accredited Social Health Activist (ASHA) program is the largest community health worker program in the world and is credited with improving the healthcare of rural Indians. However, there have been no comprehensive quantitative evaluations of this program. This paper exploits the early rollout of the ASHA program in high focus states to identify the effect of the ASHA program using a year and state fixed effects model. I find no evidence that the ASHA program had a positive effect on health inputs or health outcomes. I propose that the ASHA program's performance payment scheme may create a multitasking problem that explains this absence of a measureable effect.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
